          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had three headaches this month, all on one side, with nausea and the light bothering me.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The urgency check found no emergency pattern in its red-flag list. That is not a clearance. If anything below applies to you, or the headaches change, get medical advice promptly.\n\n**Seek emergency care now if any of these occur** (from the sudden-severe-headache guidance):\n- A headache that peaks within about a minute, often described as the worst of your life.\n- Headache with fever and a stiff neck, or with a rash of spots that do not fade when pressed.\n- Headache with new confusion, drowsiness, seizure, weakness, numbness, trouble speaking, or loss of vision.\n- Headache after a head injury, especially with vomiting, drowsiness, or getting worse over hours.\n- Headache with severe eye pain, a red eye, and blurred vision or halos around lights.\n\nYour light sensitivity matters here. The meningitis guidance lists severe dislike of bright light alongside headache and fever as an emergency sign. If you have a fever, call emergency services.\n\n**Seek same-day advice** if this is a new type of headache for you, particularly if you are over 50. The guidance also says to see a clinician soon if headaches are becoming more frequent or are not responding as usual.\n\n**What your pattern may fit.** One-sided headache with nausea and light sensitivity is the classic description of migraine, according to the general clinical reference. The same reference notes that migraine headaches are not always one-sided, which is one reason they are often misidentified. Other causes are worth raising with a clinician, not because you have them, but so they can be ruled out. Based on the material I could retrieve, these include anxiety, poor sleep, and pain-relief medication. Frequent use of pain relievers can itself keep headaches going. The guideline sets limits: fewer than ten days a month for stronger acute medicines, and fewer than fifteen for simple painkillers. Note how often you have taken any headache medicine this month and mention it to a clinician.\n\n**What to do next.** Book an appointment with a doctor or pharmacist to discuss the three headaches, your medication use, your sleep, and any stress. Keep a short diary of when each headache starts, how long it lasts, what you took, and what came with it.\n\n*Limit of this answer:* my search tool was unavailable after the first step, so I could not check further sources on less common headache causes. The points above come only from the passages I retrieved.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
